A remote MCP server reached over plain HTTP, and a host that only begins like
a loopback address. The second is why the rule parses the host rather than
matching a prefix: `127.0.0.1.example.invalid` resolves wherever its owner
says.

>>> .mcp.json
{"mcpServers":{
  "remote":{"url":"http://mcp.invalid/sse"},
  "lookalike":{"url":"http://127.0.0.1.example.invalid/sse"}
}}
